WHAT THE HOLD BREAKER DOES
--------------------------
On a vehicle at 10 km/h or more, when the RRC connection is still up 5 s after
our last datagram (checked from the fix-wait tick, which only fires when no
fix arrives within a second, i.e. in a hold): replies are taken in, anything
unanswered goes back to the backlog, the socket is closed, LTE goes off with
AT+CFUN=20 (receiver keeps running).  Records are parked in the backlog from
STATE_SEND instead of sent.  At the first batch after 20 s: CFUN=21, up to REG_S
(30 s on the car) to register, backlog out oldest first, then the live batch.  The next 4 sends
are cut straight after their replies; the one after that waits for the
network, so a unit that has left the holding cells goes back to normal.

Ends early (restored at the top of the main loop) for: key off, engine not
running, coasting, track mode, an alert or impact to send, a setting to
report (send_int_to_server), a failed send, ignition not yet reported, backlog
at 75 % (24 of 32), speed under 5 km/h.  No registration in REG_S: radio left
to the modem, feature off until the modem registers again + 5 min (0.4.83; rest of the
trip in 0.4.82; 10 min before that).  CFUN=20 refused: off for the boot.
modem_radio_up(), modem_power_off() and modem_rescan_plmn() also clear a cut.

